# Mutation plan for the four custom-status routes and the `UpdateUser` beneath them.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/custom-status-writes.plan
#
# `UserStore::update` is the security boundary of every update route in the server: thirteen
# fields are copied from the stored row onto the submitted user, and two more when the caller is
# untrusted. Without them a client could set its own password hash, mark its own email verified,
# clear its own failed-login count, turn off its own MFA, grant itself a role, or un-deactivate
# its own account by putting the field in a request body. Those copies are mutated first.
#
# **Equivalent mutants, deliberately not here.** Measured, each survives for a reason about the
# route rather than a gap in the tests:
#
#   * the `trusted_update_data` branch taken as `true`. Every api4 caller passes `false`, so
#     flipping the constant at the call site is the only reachable form and it *is* mutated
#     (`update-user-store-trusts-the-request`). Mutating inside the `if` would test the CLI path.
#   * `user.create_at = prev.create_at` in `App::update_user`. The store restores it again from
#     its own read, so the app-layer assignment cannot change what is written. Go carries the
#     same redundancy.
#   * `new_user.sanitize(...)` at the end of `App::update_user`. The store already sanitized both
#     halves; Go does it twice too.
#   * the `is_bot` exemption in the `RequireEmailVerification` branch. That flag is off on the
#     shared stack, and a `SecondServer` with it on cannot reach the branch either — no bot has a
#     password to authenticate with. Recorded rather than tested.
#   * `update-user-skips-the-group-name-check`. `isUniqueToGroupNames` runs only when the username
#     changes, and **no ported route changes a username** — the custom-status routes write `Props`
#     and nothing else. It becomes reachable with `PUT /users/{id}`, and belongs in that plan.
#   * swapping `invalid_param("recent_custom_status")` for `invalid_param("custom_status")`.
#     `AppError.Params` is `json:"-"`, so neither name reaches the client and the id and status
#     are identical. The same equivalence the team plan records for `id` vs `team_id`.
#   * `update-user-store-trusts-the-request` — flipping `update(&user, false)` to `true` at the
#     call site. The `store` lines below mutate both paths directly against
#     `db_user_update.rs`, which asserts the trusted and untrusted branches side by side; the
#     call-site flip only re-tests what `the_trusted_path_differs_in_exactly_two_fields` pins.
#
# **The store lines run against `db_user_update.rs`, not the parity suite, and that is the whole
# finding of this plan's first run.** Sixteen mutations survived because no ported route lets a
# client submit a `User`: the custom-status routes change `Props`, so deleting the password copy
# or the roles copy is invisible over HTTP. The store is the layer that can be handed a poisoned
# user, so that is where the boundary is pinned until `PUT /users/{id}` lands. Run them with:
#
#   MUTATE_STORE_TARGETS='--test db_user_update' scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/custom-status-writes.plan
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.

# --- the store's field copies: the security boundary ------------------------------------------
